      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The park’s forest boasts Douglas fir, western red cedar, and western hemlock trees some over 600 years old.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/rockport-state-park-history",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Coast Salish peoples have used the area’s cedar for clothing, basketry, dwellings, artwork, and dugout canoes on the Skagit River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/rockport-state-park-history",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1935, a land exchange traded 556 acres of state trust lands for the park’s current acreage, approved by the Board of State Land Commissioners.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/rockport-state-park-history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1971 Washington Legislature directed the sale of leased trust lands for park purposes, securing permanent protection of the timber in 1992.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/rockport-state-park-history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 2007, falling limbs from aging trees led park managers to close the campground, preserving the ancient forest.",
